 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "In 1780, a woman walked into a British armoury... on fire.",
   "emph": [
    "1780",
    "fire"
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Her name was Kuyili.",
   "emph": [
    "kuyili"
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Eight years earlier, the East India Company and its allies killed the King of Sivaganga.",
   "emph": [
    "sivaganga"
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "His queen, Velu Nachiyar, escaped.",
   "emph": [
    "velu",
    "nachiyar",
    "escaped"
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "But she didn't just hide. With help from Hyder Ali and the Maruthu brothers, she built an army.",
   "emph": [
    "army",
    "hyder",
    "maruthu"
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "When she came back for her kingdom, one deadly obstacle stood in the way: the enemy's weapons store inside the fort.",
   "emph": [
    "one",
    "deadly",
    "obstacle"
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Later accounts say that on Vijayadashami, women were allowed inside to worship.",
   "emph": [
    "vijayadashami"
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Kuyili led the queen's women soldiers right through the gates.",
   "emph": [
    "kuyili",
    "gates"
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Tradition says she soaked her clothes in ghee, set herself ablaze, and ran straight into the gunpowder.",
   "emph": [
    "ghee",
    "ablaze",
    "gunpowder"
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "The explosion destroyed the arsenal. Sivaganga was free again.",
   "emph": [
    "explosion",
    "destroyed",
    "free"
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Much of her story survives only in tradition."
  },
  {
   "text": "But now you know her name... Kuyili.",
   "emph": [
    "kuyili"
   ]
  }
 ],
